      "summary": "The proceedings record a four-day symposium at the National Physical Laboratory in Teddington, 24–27 November 1958, built on the premise that several separate specialties shared one subject. The preface lists them: artificial thinking, character and pattern recognition, learning, mechanical language translation, biology, automatic programming, industrial planning and clerical mechanization. The organizers felt the common theme was the mechanization of thought processes and that specialists in each would gain from meeting. In his opening address, NPL director G. B. B. M. Sutherland states the laboratory's own interest plainly: its Control Mechanisms and Electronics Division wanted to build equipment for routine mental work then done by large numbers of people. The first paper, Marvin Minsky's, declines to define intelligence at all and proposes heuristic programming as the working method.",
