            "id": "core-claim",
            "title": "Core Claim",
            "html": "<p>Raj Reddy, a founder and later president of AAAI, explains why American AI researchers created their own scientific society in 1979. Through the 1970s the only regular meeting for the field was the biennial International Joint Conference on AI (IJCAI), run by volunteer faculty. After organizing IJCAI-77 and IJCAI-79, Reddy concluded the community needed &quot;an enduring administrative mechanism and institutional memory&quot; of the kind the IEEE and ACM provided. Joining an existing society was possible, he writes, but &quot;the time and the effort needed to get them to accept AI was too daunting.&quot; The article describes how the society was named, financed and governed, and what its first conference (AAAI-80, at Stanford) contained.</p>",
            "text": "Raj Reddy, a founder and later president of AAAI, explains why American AI researchers created their own scientific society in 1979. Through the 1970s the only regular meeting for the field was the biennial International Joint Conference on AI (IJCAI), run by volunteer faculty. After organizing IJCAI-77 and IJCAI-79, Reddy concluded the community needed \"an enduring administrative mechanism and institutional memory\" of the kind the IEEE and ACM provided. Joining an existing society was possible, he writes, but \"the time and the effort needed to get them to accept AI was too daunting.\" The article describes how the society was named, financed and governed, and what its first conference (AAAI-80, at Stanford) contained."
