            "title": "History of Technology Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li>A clear case for Revisionist History: reverse-mode differentiation and error backpropagation had earlier formulations (Linnainmaa 1970; Werbos 1974). The 1986 paper is remembered as the origin because of where it was published and who took it up, not because it came first.</li><li>Useful against lone-inventor stories: the &quot;invention&quot; is really a moment of uptake by a relevant social group ready to use it.</li><li>The long gap between publication and dominance is a reminder that a technique needs a supporting system — data, hardware, benchmarks — before it becomes consequential.</li></ul>",
            "text": "- A clear case for Revisionist History: reverse-mode differentiation and error backpropagation had earlier formulations (Linnainmaa 1970; Werbos 1974). The 1986 paper is remembered as the origin because of where it was published and who took it up, not because it came first.\n- Useful against lone-inventor stories: the \"invention\" is really a moment of uptake by a relevant social group ready to use it.\n- The long gap between publication and dominance is a reminder that a technique needs a supporting system — data, hardware, benchmarks — before it becomes consequential."
