            "text": "- Interpretative Flexibility: Breiman offers several readings of why forests work and does not settle on one. He conjectures that Adaboost \"in its later stages\" is itself \"emulating a random forest\"; he notes forests \"may also be viewed as a Bayesian procedure\" while doubting the view; and he cites his own work treating them as \"a kernel acting on the true margin.\" His summary is plain: \"The mechanism for this is not obvious.\" The method's accuracy is fixed; its explanation stays open.\n- Closure: Breiman writes that \"the conventional thinking was that forests could not compete with arcing type algorithms in terms of accuracy,\" and that \"Our results dispel this belief.\" Within the community comparing ensemble methods, an informal consensus had formed against forests. His results reopen that judgment."
