            "html": "<ul><li>&quot;Contrary to what appears to be a widely-held intuition, our experimental results reveal that it is possible to train a face detector without having to label images as containing a face or not.&quot;</li></ul><p>The central claim, set against the field's assumption that labels are required.</p><ul><li>&quot;We conjecture that the long training time is partially responsible for the lack of high-level features reported in the literature.&quot;</li></ul><p>The scale frame: the obstacle is compute and time, not method.</p><ul><li>&quot;users of the framework merely need to write the desired upwards and downwards computation functions for the neurons in the model&quot;</li></ul><p>DistBelief hides the distributed machinery; infrastructure as the enabling layer.</p><ul><li>&quot;It is worth noting that our network is still tiny compared to the human visual cortex&quot;</li></ul><p>The authors measure their network against the brain, pointing toward further scaling.</p><ul><li>&quot;Practically, this provides an inexpensive way to develop features from unlabeled data.&quot;</li></ul><p>The economic reading of the result: unlabeled data replaces labeling labor.</p>",
