        "citation": "Mikolov, Tomas, Kai Chen, Greg Corrado, and Jeffrey Dean. 2013. \"Efficient Estimation of Word Representations in Vector Space.\" arXiv 1301.3781. January 16, 2013. https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.3781",
        "keyClaim": "Four Google researchers propose two simple architectures, Continuous Bag-of-Words (CBOW) and Skip-gram, for learning a vector for every word from very large text collections. They drop the non-linear hidden layer that makes neural language models expensive, trading some expressive power for the ability to train on billions of words. They introduce a test set of about 19,500 word-relationship questions and report that vectors trained this way answer them far better than earlier published vectors, at much lower cost: less than a day for 1.6 billion words.",
