            "id": "core-claim",
            "title": "Core Claim",
            "html": "<p>Six researchers from Google Brain, Stanford, UC Berkeley and OpenAI argue that AI safety can be studied as a set of practical machine learning problems rather than through speculation about superintelligence. They define &quot;accidents&quot; as unintended and harmful behavior that emerges from poor design of real-world AI systems, and they sort accident risk into five research problems: avoiding negative side effects, avoiding reward hacking, scalable oversight, safe exploration, and robustness to distributional shift. They argue that trends toward reinforcement learning, more complex environments and more autonomous systems will make these problems more important, and they propose experiments for each.</p>",
