        "citation": "Buolamwini, Joy, and Timnit Gebru. 2018. \"Gender Shades: Intersectional Accuracy Disparities in Commercial Gender Classification.\" Proceedings of the 1st Conference on Fairness, Accountability and Transparency, PMLR 81:77–91. Conference held February 23–24, 2018. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v81/buolamwini18a.html",
        "keyClaim": "Buolamwini and Gebru argue that the accuracy of commercial facial analysis cannot be judged by a single aggregate number. They show that two widely used face benchmarks, IJB-A and Adience, are mostly made up of lighter-skinned people, build a new benchmark balanced by gender and skin type, and use it to audit three commercial gender classifiers sold by Microsoft, IBM and Face++. All three perform worst on darker-skinned women, with error rates up to 34.7%, against at most 0.8% for lighter-skinned men. The authors conclude that inclusive benchmarks and subgroup accuracy reports are needed before companies can claim fair, transparent and accountable facial analysis.",
