            "id": "useful-quotes-evidence",
            "title": "Useful Quotes / Evidence",
            "html": "<ul><li>&quot;So today, we're announcing seven principles to guide our work going forward. These are not theoretical concepts; they are concrete standards that will actively govern our research and product development and will impact our business decisions.&quot;</li></ul><p>The post's framing of the principles as binding rather than aspirational.</p><ul><li>&quot;Weapons or other technologies whose principal purpose or implementation is to cause or directly facilitate injury to people.&quot;</li></ul><p>One of the four categories of application Google states it will not pursue.</p><ul><li>&quot;We want to be clear that while we are not developing AI for use in weapons, we will continue our work with governments and the military in many other areas.&quot;</li></ul><p>The carve-out distinguishing weapons development from other military collaboration.</p><ul><li>&quot;This approach is consistent with the values laid out in our original Founders' Letter back in 2004. There we made clear our intention to take a long-term perspective, even if it means making short-term tradeoffs. We said it then, and we believe it now.&quot;</li></ul><p>The post's framing of the 2018 principles as continuous with earlier company values.</p><ul><li>&quot;Update Feb 4, 2025: We've made updates to our AI Principles. Visit AI.Google for the latest.&quot;</li></ul><p>The editorial note prefacing the cached page, establishing that the 2018 text was later revised.</p>",
