            "id": "ai-history-notes",
            "title": "AI History Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li>GPT-2 is &quot;a direct scale-up of GPT,&quot; with more than 10 times the parameters and data; 1.5 billion parameters, trained on 8 million web pages.</li><li>Dataset: OpenAI scraped pages linked from Reddit posts with at least 3 karma, as a human-curation filter, which it says gave higher quality than datasets such as CommonCrawl.</li><li>Results: state-of-the-art scores on several language modeling benchmarks in the &quot;zero-shot&quot; setting, without training on those benchmarks' data. Downstream task scores are &quot;far from state-of-the-art.&quot;</li><li>Misuse concerns named: misleading news articles, impersonation online, automated abusive or faked social media content, spam and phishing.</li><li>February release: a smaller model with sampling code; no dataset, training code or full weights. OpenAI cites its Charter's expectation that safety concerns would &quot;reduce our traditional publishing in the future.&quot;</li><li>May 2019 update: release of a 345M-parameter model; sharing of 762M and 1.5B versions with partners in AI and security; an output dataset of about 250,000 samples per model/setting; discussions with a Partnership on AI dinner and a presentation to policymakers at the State Department's Global Engagement Center.</li><li>OpenAI also calls on governments to monitor &quot;the societal impact and diffusion of AI technologies.&quot;</li></ul>",
