            "title": "History of Technology Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li>Interpretative Flexibility: OpenAI presents the same model as useful (writing assistants, dialogue agents, translation, speech recognition) and dangerous (fake news, impersonation, spam). The release strategy is built around that ambiguity: the company says it is &quot;not sure that it is the right decision today.&quot; In 2019 the meaning of a capable text generator was open, and OpenAI chose to act on the dangerous reading while the question stayed unresolved.</li><li>Corporate Self-regulation: the decision to withhold, and the later schedule of staged releases, is made by OpenAI alone, based on factors it lists (ease of use, replication by others, evidence of misuse, demand, stakeholder input). The company invites discussion and partnerships but keeps the decision. This set a pattern for AI in which the developer decides what is safe to release.</li><li>Technological Momentum: OpenAI admits the limits of its control: &quot;some researchers have the technical capacity to reproduce and open source our results,&quot; and training systems of GPT-2-345M's capability is &quot;well within the reach of many actors already.&quot; The staged release is a delay against momentum it cannot stop; the post says it only &quot;gives the AI community more time.&quot;</li><li>Where it sits in the process: GPT-2 marks the start of a contest over release norms for large models. No closure is reached; OpenAI calls its choice an experiment and a case study.</li></ul>",
