        "title": "\"Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models\"",
        "available": true,
        "html": "<p>by Jared Kaplan, Sam McCandlish, Tom Henighan, Tom B. Brown, Benjamin Chess, Rewon Child, Scott Gray, Alec Radford, Jeffrey Wu, Dario Amodei</p><p>2020</p><p>Cross-entropy loss scalles as a power law as the model, dataset, and compute increase</p><p>As loss decreases, the predictive ability gets better</p><h4>added — sources + connections</h4><h4>source</h4><p>Jared Kaplan, Sam McCandlish, et al., &quot;Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models,&quot; 2020</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">arXiv:2001.08361</a></li><li>the later correction on compute-optimal allocation: Hoffmann et al., <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">&quot;Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models&quot;</a> (Chinchilla, 2022) — revised the model/data trade-off substantially. Useful evidence that even a &quot;law&quot; was contested and adjusted.</li></ul><h4>this is the most determinist document in the vault</h4><p>A power law is presented as a property of the world: loss falls predictably as model, data, and compute increase. The rhetorical effect is that <strong>the future becomes a budgeting question</strong>.</p><p>That makes this paper the sharpest available test case for the vault's central debate. Set it against Technological Determinism as Nye and Fischer define it:</p><ul><li>the Impact-imprint Model — a technology's technical characteristics become corresponding social characteristics. Scaling-law discourse does this constantly: <em>smooth loss curve</em> → <em>smooth, predictable social transformation</em>.</li><li>Fischer's reply in “America Calling” — Chapters 3, 8, 9: technological change was dramatic, the resulting social change more modest and uneven. A reliable curve in training loss is not a reliable curve in adoption, meaning, or consequence.</li></ul><h4>what the law actually licenses</h4><p>The empirical claim is narrow: cross-entropy loss scales as a power law in compute, parameters, and data.</p><p>The claims built on top of it are not narrow, and they are institutional:</p><p>1. <strong>capital concentration is rational</strong> — if returns are predictable, the largest spender wins, and it becomes irrational to fund small teams 2. <strong>the research agenda is settled</strong> — architecture search matters less than procurement 3. <strong>the frontier is properly private</strong> — only firms able to commit billions can participate</p><p>None of those follow from the curve. They are Constitutive Choices in Wolff's sense, defended with a technical finding.</p><h4>connections</h4><ul><li>Technological Momentum — Hughes's best concept for this paper. Momentum is <em>not</em> determinism; it is the durable result of earlier social and technical choices. Scaling laws describe momentum accurately and then misdescribe it as physics.</li><li>Naturalization — calling it a <em>law</em> is the rhetorical mechanism. Compare Marketplace Democracy in Telecommunications, Mass Media, and Democracy — Chapters 1, 5, 8, 10: the radio lobby's claim that market outcomes expressed listener sovereignty. In both cases a contested institutional arrangement is redescribed as a neutral mechanism.</li><li>Unnatural Monopoly — Wolff's term is the precise counter. Western Union's dominance was also explained at the time as the natural consequence of network economics; Wolff shows it was built through patents, contracts, courts, lobbying, and capital. Ask the identical question of compute: which part of frontier-lab concentration is cost structure, and which part is strategy?</li><li>Crisis of Control — Hughes's term for what happens when system growth outruns coordination capacity. Worth watching as a frame for AI safety and evaluation debates.</li><li>Language Models are Few-Shot Learnings — the companion demonstration</li><li>&quot;Attention is All You Need&quot; — the architecture that made the regime parallelisable, and therefore purchasable</li><li>Internalist History — this note is a compact internalist statement. The contextualist question: <em>who benefits from the claim that the curve is a law?</em> Nye's method is to use internalist evidence to reconstruct the available choices, then contextualist evidence to explain selection.</li></ul><h4>added — history-of-technology analysis (Claude draft, Sep 2026)</h4><h4>History of Technology</h4><ul><li>System Builders: every author except Kaplan lists only &quot;OpenAI&quot;; Kaplan's line reads &quot;Johns Hopkins University, OpenAI.&quot; The paper that licenses capital concentration as rational was written entirely inside the lab positioned to benefit from that conclusion — a direct answer to the contextualist question the rest of this note already poses but leaves open: who benefits from calling the curve a law is the same institution that measured it.</li><li>Co-production: the finding and its institutional purpose were built together from the start, not separated and later applied. The abstract states the loss &quot;scales as a power-law with model size, dataset size, and the amount of compute used for training,&quot; then immediately adds that &quot;these relationships allow us to determine the optimal allocation of a fixed compute budget.&quot; A budgeting tool and a scientific claim arrive in the same sentence.</li><li>Where it sits: momentum, not determinism, is what actually gets built here — a durable institutional commitment (large-scale, capital-intensive training as the default research strategy) forms around a measurement of a specific regime, at a specific lab, at a specific moment, then gets read back as if it described training in general.</li></ul><h4>Social History / SCOT</h4><ul><li>Use Stage: the paper's own claim is narrow and descriptive — a power-law fit to loss curves. Its consequential meaning is fixed later, at the point of use, when labs and investors cite the relationship to justify the capital-concentration logic the rest of this note already names. The finding's design and its institutional effect are not the same event.</li></ul><h4>Place in the AI story</h4><p>Before: &quot;Attention is All You Need&quot; (2017) supplies the parallelizable architecture this paper's compute-budget logic depends on. After: Language Models are Few-Shot Learnings (2020) is the paper's own companion demonstration, spending against the curve this note describes. Read against Synthesis — AI as Technology History, &quot;China's Moonshot, Z.AI, and DeepSeek are challenging U.S. AI labs-and beating them on cost&quot; (2026) complicates the institutional claim built on top of the law: this paper's logic said only the largest, best-capitalized labs could compete on the frontier, and constrained Chinese labs later got there on smaller budgets by engineering around the curve rather than buying past it.</p>",
