            "title": "Core Claim",
            "html": "<p>Based on three days at OpenAI's office and nearly three dozen interviews, Hao reports a gap between OpenAI's public self-description as an open, mission-driven nonprofit and what she found operating behind closed doors: a culture of secrecy, tight message control, a 2018 Charter used internally as a loyalty test tied to pay, and a still-unexplained shift toward the compute-heavy, increasingly closed research strategy that produced the withheld GPT-2 release and a secret &quot;top-secret&quot; follow-on project. She frames OpenAI's nonprofit status and its later 2019 for-profit transition as decisions made under competitive and financial pressure that the public announcements did not fully disclose.</p>",
            "text": "Based on three days at OpenAI's office and nearly three dozen interviews, Hao reports a gap between OpenAI's public self-description as an open, mission-driven nonprofit and what she found operating behind closed doors: a culture of secrecy, tight message control, a 2018 Charter used internally as a loyalty test tied to pay, and a still-unexplained shift toward the compute-heavy, increasingly closed research strategy that produced the withheld GPT-2 release and a secret \"top-secret\" follow-on project. She frames OpenAI's nonprofit status and its later 2019 for-profit transition as decisions made under competitive and financial pressure that the public announcements did not fully disclose."
