        "citation": "Hendrycks, Dan, Collin Burns, Steven Basart, Andy Zou, Mantas Mazeika, Dawn Song, and Jacob Steinhardt. 2020. \"Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding.\" arXiv 2009.03300. September 7, 2020. Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2021. https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.03300",
        "keyClaim": "Hendrycks and colleagues argue that existing language benchmarks no longer measure what large pretrained models can actually do. GLUE and SuperGLUE were each matched at human level within about a year, yet the authors hold that models remain far from human language understanding \"as a whole.\" Their answer is a 57-subject multiple-choice test, from elementary mathematics to professional law and medicine, taken only in zero-shot and few-shot settings, so that it measures knowledge picked up during pretraining rather than skill at a fine-tuning set. The result they report: most models sit at random chance, the 175-billion-parameter GPT-3 reaches 43.9%, and no model reaches expert level on any s",
