          {
            "id": "the-central-claim-in-vault-vocabulary",
            "title": "the central claim, in vault vocabulary",
            "html": "<p>AI is treated as an extension of <strong>managerial control</strong> — the question to ask of any AI system is <em>what is being managed, and on whose behalf</em>: populations, students, resources, images, livestock, office work, diagnosis, discourse.</p><p>That reframes AI from an artifact into a system and then into a technological culture — exactly the outward movement of units of analysis mapped in &quot;How is technology made?—That is the question!&quot;.</p>",
            "text": "AI is treated as an extension of managerial control — the question to ask of any AI system is what is being managed, and on whose behalf: populations, students, resources, images, livestock, office work, diagnosis, discourse.\n\nThat reframes AI from an artifact into a system and then into a technological culture — exactly the outward movement of units of analysis mapped in \"How is technology made?—That is the question!\"."
          },
          {
            "id": "connections",
            "title": "connections",
            "html": "<ul><li>Revisionist History — the collection's rejection of the two standard origin myths (Turing 1950, Dartmouth 1956) is structurally the same move McChesney makes against both the industry history <em>and</em> the critical history of broadcasting in Telecommunications, Mass Media, and Democracy — Chapters 1, 5, 8, 10. Both erase conflict; both make the outcome look settled earlier than it was.</li><li>Internalist History — the note's observation that early AI histories &quot;were reflections by practitioners thinking about its methods&quot; is the classic internalist condition: the field writing its own history from the inside, with the technical sequence foregrounded and the institutions invisible.</li><li>Interpretative Flexibility — the debate the note records over whether intelligence is social and embodied or reducible to formalism <em>is</em> interpretative flexibility, still open, about the field's own object.</li><li>three paradigms → three different technologies, not three stages of one:</li><li><strong>symbolic manipulation</strong> → A PROPOSAL FOR THE DARTMOUTH SUMMER RESEARCH PROJECTON ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE</li><li><strong>expert systems</strong> (if-then knowledge bases) → no primary source in the vault yet; this is the clearest gap</li><li><strong>data-driven machine learning</strong> → ImageNet Classification with Deep Convolutional Neural Networks, The data that transformed AI research-and possibly the world, &quot;Attention is All You Need&quot;</li><li>Fischer's warning in “America Calling” — Chapters 3, 8, 9 against the Symptomatic Approach applies here: do not treat these as three expressions of one underlying thing called &quot;AI.&quot; They had different funders, different institutions, different failure modes, and different publics.</li><li><strong>colonisation and extraction</strong> — the framing you noted at the end connects directly to the labour and data-sourcing story in The data that transformed AI research-and possibly the world, and to Structure in Fischer's sense: who supplies the inputs, on what terms, and who is absent from the archive.</li><li>Co-production — the &quot;genealogy of power&quot; framing is a co-production claim: AI systems and the administrative orders they serve are made together, and neither is prior.</li></ul>",
            "text": "- Revisionist History — the collection's rejection of the two standard origin myths (Turing 1950, Dartmouth 1956) is structurally the same move McChesney makes against both the industry history and the critical history of broadcasting in Telecommunications, Mass Media, and Democracy — Chapters 1, 5, 8, 10. Both erase conflict; both make the outcome look settled earlier than it was.\n- Internalist History — the note's observation that early AI histories \"were reflections by practitioners thinking about its methods\" is the classic internalist condition: the field writing its own history from the inside, with the technical sequence foregrounded and the institutions invisible.\n- Interpretative Flexibility — the debate the note records over whether intelligence is social and embodied or reducible to formalism is interpretative flexibility, still open, about the field's own object.\n- three para"
          },
          {
            "id": "what-to-do-with-this-note",
            "title": "what to do with this note",
            "html": "<p>Use it as the lens for re-reading every technical paper in the vault. For each one ask the collection's question: <em>what is being managed here, by whom, and what older managerial practice is being automated?</em></p><p>That question is developed further in Synthesis — AI as Technology History.</p>",
            "text": "Use it as the lens for re-reading every technical paper in the vault. For each one ask the collection's question: what is being managed here, by whom, and what older managerial practice is being automated?\n\nThat question is developed further in Synthesis — AI as Technology History."
          }
