            "html": "<ul><li>As history of technology, the article shows that invention is not only a matter of creating the largest or most advanced artifact. It also depends on reproducibility, circulation, modification, cost reduction, and the ability of many actors to experiment.</li><li>The leaked document treats LoRA, quantization, and smaller models as infrastructural changes: they lowered the cost of participation and changed who could contribute to AI development.</li><li>The article challenges a linear progress story in which larger models automatically defeat smaller ones. Instead, it argues that faster iteration, easier customization, and distributed improvement can compete with centralized scale.</li><li>It connects AI to larger transitions in computing history: from mainframe-like centralization to personal computing, from proprietary software to open-source ecosystems, and from closed research labs to platform-based innovation.</li></ul>",
            "text": "- As history of technology, the article shows that invention is not only a matter of creating the largest or most advanced artifact. It also depends on reproducibility, circulation, modification, cost reduction, and the ability of many actors to experiment.\n- The leaked document treats LoRA, quantization, and smaller models as infrastructural changes: they lowered the cost of participation and changed who could contribute to AI development.\n- The article challenges a linear progress story in which larger models automatically defeat smaller ones. Instead, it argues that faster iteration, easier customization, and distributed improvement can compete with centralized scale.\n- It connects AI to larger transitions in computing history: from mainframe-like centralization to personal computing, from proprietary software to open-source ecosystems, and from closed research labs to platform-based "
