            "id": "social-history-scot-notes",
            "title": "Social History / SCOT Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li>Relevant social groups include Google researchers, OpenAI, Meta, open-source developers, university labs, hobbyists, model users, companies building products, dataset builders, and online communities sharing models and evaluations.</li><li>Different groups attached different meanings to LLMs. For Google and OpenAI, models were strategic assets and controlled products. For open-source communities, they became modifiable tools, local infrastructure, creative media, and objects of collective experimentation.</li><li>The problem of AI progress is defined differently by different actors. Large firms emphasize safety, product control, and competitive advantage; open-source participants emphasize access, customization, speed, and practical experimentation.</li><li>Labor appears in two ways: first, as corporate research labor that can move between firms, weakening secrecy; second, as unpaid or loosely coordinated community labor that produces adapters, datasets, evaluations, interfaces, and model variants.</li><li>The article is useful for SCOT because it shows interpretive flexibility around &quot;openness,&quot; &quot;responsible release,&quot; &quot;quality,&quot; and &quot;capability.&quot; It also shows a process of stabilization, where techniques such as LoRA and quantization quickly became accepted routes for making LLMs usable outside major labs.</li></ul>",
            "text": "- Relevant social groups include Google researchers, OpenAI, Meta, open-source developers, university labs, hobbyists, model users, companies building products, dataset builders, and online communities sharing models and evaluations.\n- Different groups attached different meanings to LLMs. For Google and OpenAI, models were strategic assets and controlled products. For open-source communities, they became modifiable tools, local infrastructure, creative media, and objects of collective experimentation.\n- The problem of AI progress is defined differently by different actors. Large firms emphasize safety, product control, and competitive advantage; open-source participants emphasize access, customization, speed, and practical experimentation.\n- Labor appears in two ways: first, as corporate research labor that can move between firms, weakening secrecy; second, as unpaid or loosely coordinat"
