            "title": "History of Technology",
            "html": "<ul><li>Unnatural Monopoly: the memo's argument is that Google and OpenAI's presumed scale advantage was never a natural monopoly at all: &quot;We have no secret sauce. Our best hope is to learn from and collaborate with what others are doing outside Google.&quot; What looked like an inherent barrier — compute, parameter count — turns out to be contingent, and the memo treats the giant-model strategy as now a liability: &quot;Giant models are slowing us down.&quot;</li><li>Path Dependence: the memo warns that once community fine-tunes accumulate on a released base model, retraining from scratch becomes the costly option: &quot;training giant models from scratch not only throws away the pretraining, but also any iterative improvements that have been made on top.&quot; Whichever model gets open-sourced fixes whose accumulated work the field builds on next.</li><li>Where it sits: closure is being lost, not reached. The memo documents a system — proprietary frontier LLMs — that looked stabilized in early 2023 coming apart within weeks: &quot;barely a month later&quot; LLaMA had spawned instruction-tuned, quantized, and RLHF'd variants across a dozen independent projects.</li></ul>",
