            "id": "history-of-technology-notes",
            "title": "History of Technology Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li>Statutory Consolidation: this hearing is the argument over the stage the vault's synthesis describes as still open for AI. Altman proposes the specific shape a new agency would take — licensing tied to a &quot;certain scale of capabilities,&quot; with revocation power — while Montgomery argues against creating any new agency at all. Nothing is decided; what the transcript shows is which design each interested party is trying to get written into law before the argument closes.</li><li>Corporate Self-regulation: Montgomery cites IBM's AI ethics board and &quot;precision regulation&quot; framing as reasons a new agency is unnecessary, explicitly grounding this in the claim that &quot;society grants our license to operate.&quot; McChesney's test is what such governance costs its author when it conflicts with revenue; the hearing itself does not test that, but Montgomery's position — internal governance in place of external structural authority — is the same move the vault's concept describes: it substitutes company process for statute while leaving the underlying question of who decides unresolved.</li><li>Agenda Control: Montgomery's &quot;precision regulation&quot; argument does more than propose a policy; it tries to define which question is even on the table — not whether an agency should exist over the technology, but which specific use cases existing regulators should act on. Graham's repeated, blunt yes/no questioning (&quot;Should you get a license to produce one of these tools?&quot;) is visibly an attempt to force that frame back open.</li><li>Crisis of Control: the specific mechanisms proposed by Altman and Marcus — audits, evaluations, a monitoring agency with recall authority — are the standard response Hughes's concept predicts once a system's growth outpaces existing coordination capacity: more system, in the form of evaluation infrastructure and standards bodies, rather than a change to the system's direction.</li></ul>",
            "text": "- Statutory Consolidation: this hearing is the argument over the stage the vault's synthesis describes as still open for AI. Altman proposes the specific shape a new agency would take — licensing tied to a \"certain scale of capabilities,\" with revocation power — while Montgomery argues against creating any new agency at all. Nothing is decided; what the transcript shows is which design each interested party is trying to get written into law before the argument closes.\n- Corporate Self-regulation: Montgomery cites IBM's AI ethics board and \"precision regulation\" framing as reasons a new agency is unnecessary, explicitly grounding this in the claim that \"society grants our license to operate.\" McChesney's test is what such governance costs its author when it conflicts with revenue; the hearing itself does not test that, but Montgomery's position — internal governance in place of external s"
