            "id": "social-history-scot-notes",
            "title": "Social History / SCOT Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li>Relevant Social Groups: the hearing puts three groups with different stakes in the same room — an AI lab that would be regulated, an established computing firm arguing for a lighter-touch model, and an academic critic with no product to protect — and their proposals differ in ways that track those interests: Altman's licensing scheme create a legal category (&quot;above a certain scale&quot;) that only frontier labs like his would fall into; Montgomery's use-case model leaves IBM's existing enterprise products largely where they are; Marcus, the only witness without a product at stake, proposes the most externally adversarial mechanism (recall authority, independent scientists with pre-release access).</li><li>Public Interest: Blumenthal's opening statement invokes &quot;sensible safeguards&quot; and &quot;public trust&quot; without defining either against a specific alternative distribution of power, in the same undefined register the vault's note on the concept flags. Altman's &quot;unconstrained by a need to generate financial return&quot; is quoted back at him by Marcus as an example of mission language that did not survive contact with the company's later structure.</li></ul>",
            "text": "- Relevant Social Groups: the hearing puts three groups with different stakes in the same room — an AI lab that would be regulated, an established computing firm arguing for a lighter-touch model, and an academic critic with no product to protect — and their proposals differ in ways that track those interests: Altman's licensing scheme create a legal category (\"above a certain scale\") that only frontier labs like his would fall into; Montgomery's use-case model leaves IBM's existing enterprise products largely where they are; Marcus, the only witness without a product at stake, proposes the most externally adversarial mechanism (recall authority, independent scientists with pre-release access).\n- Public Interest: Blumenthal's opening statement invokes \"sensible safeguards\" and \"public trust\" without defining either against a specific alternative distribution of power, in the same undefin"
