            "html": "<ul><li>&quot;By itself, GPT-3 is not a high-risk system&quot;</li></ul><p>OpenAI's white paper to EU officials, arguing against classifying its general-purpose systems in the Act's strictest category.</p><ul><li>&quot;What they're saying is basically: trust us to self-regulate&quot;</li></ul><p>Daniel Leufer of Access Now, describing the underlying logic of OpenAI's white paper.</p><ul><li>&quot;They got what they asked for&quot;</li></ul><p>Sarah Chander of European Digital Rights, on the final Act's exclusion of the amendment OpenAI had objected to.</p><ul><li>&quot;OpenAI did not tell us what good regulation should look like&quot;</li></ul><p>A European Commission source's account of OpenAI's June 2022 meeting with Commission officials.</p><ul><li>&quot;shows that OpenAI, like many Big Tech companies, have used the argument of utility and public benefit of AI to mask their financial interest in watering down the regulation&quot;</li></ul><p>Chander's assessment of what the white paper demonstrates.</p>",
            "text": "- \"By itself, GPT-3 is not a high-risk system\"\n  OpenAI's white paper to EU officials, arguing against classifying its general-purpose systems in the Act's strictest category.\n\n- \"What they're saying is basically: trust us to self-regulate\"\n  Daniel Leufer of Access Now, describing the underlying logic of OpenAI's white paper.\n\n- \"They got what they asked for\"\n  Sarah Chander of European Digital Rights, on the final Act's exclusion of the amendment OpenAI had objected to.\n\n- \"OpenAI did not tell us what good regulation should look like\"\n  A European Commission source's account of OpenAI's June 2022 meeting with Commission officials.\n\n- \"shows that OpenAI, like many Big Tech companies, have used the argument of utility and public benefit of AI to mask their financial interest in watering down the regulation\"\n  Chander's assessment of what the white paper demonstrates."
