            "title": "History of Technology Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li>Corporate Self-regulation: this document is close to a pure instance of the vault's concept — internal protocols, adopted and revisable by Anthropic's own board, published in the same register as an external regulatory commitment. Anthropic's own framing makes the political function explicit: the RSP is offered as something that &quot;should be seen as analogous to the pre-market testing&quot; conducted in already-regulated industries, at the same time Congress had not yet built any equivalent binding requirement for AI. McChesney's test — what does the commitment cost its author when it conflicts with revenue — is not answerable from this announcement alone, since the piece states plainly that the RSP &quot;will not alter current uses of Claude or disrupt availability of our products.&quot;</li><li>Statutory Consolidation: this policy substitutes a company's own board-approved protocol for the kind of dedicated regulatory authority discussed, but not created, in &quot;Oversight of A.I. - Rules for Artificial Intelligence&quot; the previous May. Publishing detailed capability thresholds (ASL-1 through ASL-4) before any external body required them is a way of arriving at the content of statutory consolidation without its enforcement mechanism — the standards exist, but Anthropic's own board, not an external agency, is the body that can revise or waive them.</li><li>Agenda Control: Anthropic states directly that it hopes the ASL framework will be &quot;adopted as a standard across frontier labs,&quot; which is an attempt to set the terms of an industry-wide or eventually regulatory framework from inside a single company, before any legislature or agency has settled what the terms should be. Publishing first, in detail, is itself a way of shaping what any later external standard is likely to look like.</li></ul>",
