            "id": "why-this-matters",
            "title": "Why This Matters",
            "html": "<p>Read against Synthesis — AI as Technology History §4.6, this complaint is evidence that by the end of 2023 the courts, not Congress, had become the venue where AI's founding practices met binding law. Every other document drafted for this course week — the executive order, the voluntary commitments, the Bletchley Declaration — is advisory, delegated, or aspirational. This one asks a federal judge to decide, under a 1976 statute, whether the core method of building a large language model is lawful. Whatever the eventual ruling, the complaint itself demonstrates that the question of who owns the inputs could not wait for AI-specific legislation to be resolved.</p>",
            "text": "Read against Synthesis — AI as Technology History §4.6, this complaint is evidence that by the end of 2023 the courts, not Congress, had become the venue where AI's founding practices met binding law. Every other document drafted for this course week — the executive order, the voluntary commitments, the Bletchley Declaration — is advisory, delegated, or aspirational. This one asks a federal judge to decide, under a 1976 statute, whether the core method of building a large language model is lawful. Whatever the eventual ruling, the complaint itself demonstrates that the question of who owns the inputs could not wait for AI-specific legislation to be resolved."
