            "title": "History of Technology Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li><strong>System Builders</strong>: the sixteen companies named in the Frontier AI Safety Commitments are, by the commitments' own definition, the actors whose systems currently set the &quot;frontier&quot; — the group both authors and is bound by the standard, since &quot;frontier AI&quot; is defined relative to &quot;the capabilities present in the most advanced models,&quot; which are these companies' own products.</li><li><strong>Agenda Control</strong>: the commitments ask companies to define their own risk thresholds — &quot;defined with input from trusted actors, including organisations' respective home governments as appropriate&quot; — with government involvement explicitly conditional rather than mandatory, and to self-report on external oversight only insofar as they choose (&quot;explain how, if at all&quot;).</li><li><strong>Corporate Self-regulation</strong>: the entire mechanism runs through company-authored safety frameworks published on a company-chosen timeline, with compliance demonstrated by the companies themselves rather than assessed by an independent body named in the text.</li><li><strong>Where this sits in the process</strong>: this document extends the pattern the Bletchley Declaration and Voluntary AI Commitments both establish — named companies commit to future self-published documents rather than accepting rules set by government or an outside body. It is not Statutory Consolidation; it is the same voluntary-commitment structure, now with a fixed public deadline attached.</li></ul>",
            "text": "- System Builders: the sixteen companies named in the Frontier AI Safety Commitments are, by the commitments' own definition, the actors whose systems currently set the \"frontier\" — the group both authors and is bound by the standard, since \"frontier AI\" is defined relative to \"the capabilities present in the most advanced models,\" which are these companies' own products.\n- Agenda Control: the commitments ask companies to define their own risk thresholds — \"defined with input from trusted actors, including organisations' respective home governments as appropriate\" — with government involvement explicitly conditional rather than mandatory, and to self-report on external oversight only insofar as they choose (\"explain how, if at all\").\n- Corporate Self-regulation: the entire mechanism runs through company-authored safety frameworks published on a company-chosen timeline, with compliance de"
