            "html": "<p>Thompson explains, in question-and-answer form, what DeepSeek actually announced over the preceding weeks — the V3 base model's low training cost, the R1 reasoning model, and the engineering behind both — and argues that the market panic of January 27 was a reaction to what the news implied about the U.S.-China AI race and about Nvidia's moat, not to any single surprising technical fact. He is explicit that the number everyone fixated on, the roughly $5.576 million training-cost figure, was reported by DeepSeek itself as covering only the final training run, and he defends it as plausible rather than treating it as the full cost of building the company. His larger argument is that the chip ban forced DeepSeek's engineers into memory-bandwidth-constrained hardware, which drove the specific architectural choices that made the low cost possible, and that this same constraint now threatens Nvidia's CUDA-and-interconnect advantage by demonstrating that heavy optimization is a substitute for buying more chips.</p>",
