            "id": "ai-history-notes",
            "title": "AI History Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li>Vance opens by naming the shift directly: he is &quot;not here this morning to talk about AI safety, which was the title of the conference a couple of years ago,&quot; but &quot;AI opportunity&quot; — an explicit renaming of what the summit itself is understood to be about.</li><li>He lists four points: American AI as &quot;the gold standard worldwide&quot;; opposition to &quot;excessive regulation of the... AI sector,&quot; which he says risks killing a transformative industry early; AI that &quot;must remain free from ideological bias&quot; and will &quot;not be co-opted into a tool for authoritarian censorship&quot;; and a &quot;pro-worker growth path.&quot;</li><li>He references &quot;the president's recent executive order on AI&quot; and states the administration is &quot;developing an AI Action Plan that avoids an overly precautionary regulatory regime&quot; — directly anticipating the document that becomes this week's other primary source, five months before it is issued.</li><li>He names EU rules specifically: the &quot;Digital Services Act&quot; and &quot;GDPR,&quot; calling their compliance costs a reason some firms &quot;block EU users in the first place.&quot;</li><li>He cites a $700 billion global AI spending estimate for 2028, saying &quot;over half of it will likely be invested in the United States.&quot;</li><li>He reframes who counts as a credible advocate for safety regulation: &quot;when a massive incumbent comes to us asking us for safety regulations, we ought to ask whether that safety regulation is for the benefit of our people or whether it's for the benefit of the incumbent.&quot;</li><li>Where this sits in the process: this is a state actor attempting to reopen, by renaming, a question the prior year's international gathering had treated as settled — not through a counter-argument on the merits of frontier-model risk, but by declaring the topic itself out of date.</li></ul>",
            "text": "- Vance opens by naming the shift directly: he is \"not here this morning to talk about AI safety, which was the title of the conference a couple of years ago,\" but \"AI opportunity\" — an explicit renaming of what the summit itself is understood to be about.\n- He lists four points: American AI as \"the gold standard worldwide\"; opposition to \"excessive regulation of the... AI sector,\" which he says risks killing a transformative industry early; AI that \"must remain free from ideological bias\" and will \"not be co-opted into a tool for authoritarian censorship\"; and a \"pro-worker growth path.\"\n- He references \"the president's recent executive order on AI\" and states the administration is \"developing an AI Action Plan that avoids an overly precautionary regulatory regime\" — directly anticipating the document that becomes this week's other primary source, five months before it is issued.\n- He n"
