        "citation": "Kwa, Thomas, Ben West, Joel Becker, et al. 2025. \"Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks.\" arXiv. March 18, 2025. https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14499. Updated in METR, \"Time Horizon 1.1,\" METR Blog, January 29, 2026, https://metr.org/blog/2026-1-29-time-horizon-1-1/",
        "keyClaim": "METR proposes the 50%-task-completion \"time horizon\" — the length of task, measured in the time a skilled human would need, that a model can complete with 50% success — as a metric to convert AI benchmark performance into a human-comparable unit, and reports that frontier time horizon \"has been doubling approximately every seven months since 2019\". Ten months later, in Time Horizon 1.1, METR revises its own task suite and infrastructure, and reports that the doubling time it measures for the post-2023 period changes from 165 days under the original methodology to 131 days under the revised one — a correction to the metric's own headline trend, published and explained in the open rather than ",
