            "title": "Useful Quotes / Evidence",
            "html": "<ul><li>&quot;Getty Images do not say that Stable Diffusion is itself a copy of, or that it stores within it any copies of, the Copyright Works.&quot;</li></ul><p>The scope of the claim Getty was left arguing after abandoning its broader allegations.</p><ul><li>&quot;Inference does not require the use of any training data and the model itself does not store training data.&quot;</li></ul><p>The court's technical description of the model, underpinning its legal conclusion.</p><ul><li>&quot;the model weights are not themselves an infringing copy and they do not store an infringing copy. They are purely the product of the patterns and features which they have learnt over time during the training process.&quot;</li></ul><p>The central holding on model weights.</p><ul><li>&quot;Getty Images' central submission that “as soon as it is made, the AI model is an infringing copy” is, accordingly, in my judgment, entirely misconceived.&quot;</li></ul><p>The court's rejection of Getty's core legal theory.</p><ul><li>&quot;there will have been many downloads of Stable Diffusion v2.x and SD XL on to computers in the UK (perhaps running into millions)&quot;</li></ul><p>The court's factual finding on distribution, offered only as an alternative holding.</p>",
            "text": "- \"Getty Images do not say that Stable Diffusion is itself a copy of, or that it stores within it any copies of, the Copyright Works.\"\n  The scope of the claim Getty was left arguing after abandoning its broader allegations.\n\n- \"Inference does not require the use of any training data and the model itself does not store training data.\"\n  The court's technical description of the model, underpinning its legal conclusion.\n\n- \"the model weights are not themselves an infringing copy and they do not store an infringing copy. They are purely the product of the patterns and features which they have learnt over time during the training process.\"\n  The central holding on model weights.\n\n- \"Getty Images' central submission that “as soon as it is made, the AI model is an infringing copy” is, accordingly, in my judgment, entirely misconceived.\"\n  The court's rejection of Getty's core legal theory.\n\n- "
