            "id": "useful-quotes-evidence",
            "title": "Useful Quotes / Evidence",
            "html": "<ul><li>&quot;Neither the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure nor the Local Civil Rules permit any party to make its assertion of privilege a moving target.&quot;</li></ul><p>Wang's rule for why OpenAI's shifting position on its own is a waiver.</p><ul><li>&quot;attorney-client privilege does not protect facts, even when they are shared with an attorney in otherwise privileged communications&quot;</li></ul><p>Wang's holding that privilege protects communications, not the underlying facts a party discussed with a lawyer.</p><ul><li>&quot;A jury is entitled to know the basis for OpenAI's purported good faith.&quot;</li></ul><p>Wang's statement of the at-issue waiver: a defendant cannot deny willfulness and then block inquiry into why it believed its conduct was lawful.</p><ul><li>&quot;voluntarily submitted their communications&quot;</li></ul><p>Judge Stein's basis, per the National Law Review account, for distinguishing ChatGPT users from wiretap subjects when ordering production of their logs.</p><ul><li>&quot;Such piracy of otherwise available copies is inherently, irredeemably infringing even if the pirated copies are immediately used for the transformative use and immediately discarded.&quot;</li></ul><p>Judge Alsup's language from Bartz, quoted by Wang against OpenAI's attempt to use that ruling in its own defense.</p>",
            "text": "- \"Neither the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure nor the Local Civil Rules permit any party to make its assertion of privilege a moving target.\"\n  Wang's rule for why OpenAI's shifting position on its own is a waiver.\n\n- \"attorney-client privilege does not protect facts, even when they are shared with an attorney in otherwise privileged communications\"\n  Wang's holding that privilege protects communications, not the underlying facts a party discussed with a lawyer.\n\n- \"A jury is entitled to know the basis for OpenAI's purported good faith.\"\n  Wang's statement of the at-issue waiver: a defendant cannot deny willfulness and then block inquiry into why it believed its conduct was lawful.\n\n- \"voluntarily submitted their communications\"\n  Judge Stein's basis, per the National Law Review account, for distinguishing ChatGPT users from wiretap subjects when ordering production of their logs.\n\n- \""
