        "citation": "Wang, Ona T. 2025. \"Opinion & Order Re: OpenAI's Deletion of Books1 and Books2 Datasets and Privilege Rulings.\" In re: OpenAI, Inc., Copyright Infringement Litigation, 25-md-3143 (S.D.N.Y.). November 24, 2025. https://www.nysd.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/2026-01/25md3143%20-%2011.24.25%20Order%20re%20ECF%20Nos.%20413,%20428,%20479,%20504,%20615.pdf",
        "keyClaim": "Magistrate Judge Ona T. Wang rules that OpenAI has waived attorney-client privilege over its reasons for deleting the Books1 and Books2 training datasets in 2022 — datasets built from pirated books downloaded from Library Genesis. OpenAI first said the deletion was \"due to non-use,\" then asserted every reason was privileged, then reversed again once plaintiffs got a discovery order. Wang finds that shifting position, on its own, a waiver, and separately finds that OpenAI's denial of willful infringement puts its state of mind \"at issue,\" which forfeits privilege over the same communications regardless of what OpenAI says it will argue at trial. She orders production of the withheld communica",
