            "html": "<p>This order is the point where the reversal this week traces stops being rhetorical and becomes procedural: a litigation task force with a thirty-day deadline, funding conditions with a ninety-day deadline, and two federal agencies directed to build preemption records, all before Congress has voted on anything. Read against Synthesis — AI as Technology History §4.6, this is exactly the &quot;open&quot; stage of statutory consolidation McChesney's history warns moves fast and without much public notice — litigation and agency proceedings substituting for the vote, while the order's own language asks Congress for the vote it has not yet taken. The gap between the order's working URL slug and its issued title is a small instance of the same gap: what the order does and what it is formally called are not quite the same thing.</p>",
            "text": "This order is the point where the reversal this week traces stops being rhetorical and becomes procedural: a litigation task force with a thirty-day deadline, funding conditions with a ninety-day deadline, and two federal agencies directed to build preemption records, all before Congress has voted on anything. Read against Synthesis — AI as Technology History §4.6, this is exactly the \"open\" stage of statutory consolidation McChesney's history warns moves fast and without much public notice — litigation and agency proceedings substituting for the vote, while the order's own language asks Congress for the vote it has not yet taken. The gap between the order's working URL slug and its issued title is a small instance of the same gap: what the order does and what it is formally called are not quite the same thing."
