            "html": "<ul><li>DeepSeek-V4-Pro has 1.6T total parameters with 49B activated per token; DeepSeek-V4-Flash has 284B total with 13B activated. Both support a context length of one million tokens, and both are described as a &quot;preview version&quot; of the V4 series.</li><li>The headline efficiency claim is stated precisely: &quot;In the 1M-token context setting, DeepSeek-V4-Pro requires only 27% of single-token inference FLOPs and 10% of KV cache compared with DeepSeek-V3.2&quot; — an explicit comparison to the company's own prior release, not to any competitor.</li><li>Two other architectural changes are named: Manifold-Constrained Hyper-Connections (mHC), described as strengthening residual connections for training stability, and the Muon optimizer, used for faster convergence.</li><li>Pre-training used more than 32 trillion tokens, roughly double DeepSeek-V3's 14.8 trillion, and post-training combines supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning with GRPO across separate domain experts, later consolidated into one model through on-policy distillation.</li><li>The instruct models expose three reasoning-effort modes (Non-think, Think High, Think Max); in the Pro-Max configuration, the card reports DeepSeek-V4-Pro ahead of Opus-4.6 Max, GPT-5.4 xHigh, and other named frontier models on several agentic and coding benchmarks (LiveCodeBench, Codeforces, Apex Shortlist), while trailing on others (GPQA Diamond, HLE, MRCR 1M).</li><li>Where this sits in the AI story: this is a direct sequel to the training-cost argument in &quot;DeepSeek-V3 Technical Report&quot;, but the efficiency claim has moved from training cost to inference cost and context-length cost, and the release mechanism (MIT license, full weights on Hugging Face) is unchanged from V3 even as competitors in the same week's readings move toward closed API access.</li></ul>",
