            "title": "History of Technology Notes",
            "html": "<ul><li>Interpretative Flexibility: TechCrunch uses &quot;annualized revenue run rate&quot; as a shared unit to rank Anthropic against OpenAI, then concedes in the same paragraph that &quot;the two companies may calculate their revenue metrics differently.&quot; The article does not say how the numbers are built, so a phrase functioning as a settled comparison is, on its own account, still interpretively open — the comparison happens before the meaning of the term is agreed.</li><li>Naturalization: the headline (per the reading's title) states the figure as an accomplished fact, while the body concedes it is &quot;a projection of a full year's revenue based on a recent, shorter period.&quot; Treating a projection as a reported outcome, rather than flagging it as an estimate throughout, is the naturalizing move: the provisional becomes, in the framing, the actual.</li><li>Black Box: readers get the output (a dollar figure, reported via Bloomberg at second hand) without the inputs — what contracts, time window, or accounting convention produced it. The number circulates as settled while its construction stays unexamined in this article.</li><li>Where this sits in the process: this is closure being attempted on a contested metric — the run-rate figure functions to settle which company is ahead without settling what is being measured, which is exactly the gap the article names but does not close.</li></ul>",
